WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.279
Since his debut in 1962, Spider-Man has been
one of the most popular superheroes in the

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:07.549
world, and he's also one of the most well-known.

00:00:07.549 --> 00:00:11.930
But with countless Spider-Man stories built
around some pretty confusing plot points,

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.590
it’s easy to get mixed up.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.640
Let us have the great responsibility of clearing
up a lot of incorrect things people believe

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.640
about Spider-Man.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:19.640
Stan Lee's most enduring classics?

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.490
Of all the original Marvel Universe titles,
The Amazing Spider-Man is the one most strongly

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:26.259
identified with Stan "The Man" Lee.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:30.689
Even though he’d be a part of all of Marvel’s
early string of hits, including the Avengers,

00:00:30.689 --> 00:00:35.250
the X-Men, the Hulk, and more, Spidey was
the character he stuck with the longest, scripting

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.420
the first 100 issues and then staying on sporadically
for another few years after.

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:46.820
"You know, I guess one person can make a difference.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:47.820
'Nuff said."

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:52.370
If, however, you think Stan's the only one
responsible for the thrills of those early

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:53.970
adventures, you're mistaken.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:57.990
Most fans are aware that Lee pioneered the
"Marvel Style" of writing, in which he'd collaborate

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:02.290
with an artist on a loose plot, then add in
the dialogue after everything else was done.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:06.409
What's a little less well-known, however,
is that his relationship with artist and Spider-Man’s

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:09.710
co-creator Steve Ditko was one of the first
to deteriorate.

00:01:09.710 --> 00:01:14.820
According to Marvel Comics: The Untold Story,
the latter chunk of Ditko's 38-issue run,

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.749
including the legendary story that gave the
world the iconic image of Spider-Man fighting

00:01:18.749 --> 00:01:23.299
his way out from under a massive heap of rubble,
was produced without Ditko and Lee speaking

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:33.590
to each other at all.

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:36.889
Of course, that doesn't mean Lee wasn't a
necessary ingredient for Spider-Man's early

00:01:36.889 --> 00:01:37.889
success.

00:01:37.889 --> 00:01:41.659
The fact that he stayed with the character
for almost seven years after Ditko's departure

00:01:41.659 --> 00:01:45.909
and kept on producing some of the best years
of Spider-Man is a testament to how important

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:50.270
he was to the character, and how well he got
along with Ditko's replacement, Jazzy John

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:51.270
Romita.

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:52.270
The Spectacularly Singular Peter Parker?

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.350
The one thing about Spider-Man that's always
been true is that underneath that mask, it's

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:57.060
always Peter Parker.

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.729
Just an average, everyday scientific super-genius
with problems just like ours.

00:02:00.729 --> 00:02:05.700
You know, like juggling our long-term relationships
to supermodels while making a living off selfies.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.009
That’s why it’s always been Peter… except
for all those times it's someone else.

00:02:10.009 --> 00:02:14.010
While Peter Parker has certainly been the
main man in the mask as Spider-Man since Spidey's

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.500
debut in 1962, he's definitely not the only
one.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:22.379
For a while in the '90s, Spider-Man was instead
Ben Reilly, a clone of Peter made by a villain

00:02:22.379 --> 00:02:23.709
called the Jackal.

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:27.480
Right around the time Peter temporarily lost
his powers, Reilly was tricked into thinking

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.040
he was the genuine article.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.349
He dyed his hair blonde, got a job at a coffee
shop, and took over the role with a new costume

00:02:33.349 --> 00:02:38.200
for the entirety of 1996 before eventually
dissolving into a pile of dust.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:43.150
More recently, Peter found his own consciousness
overwritten by Doctor Octopus, who took over

00:02:43.150 --> 00:02:45.150
his life as the Superior Spider-Man.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.340
Beyond just variations on Peter Parker's body,
and enough alternate universe versions that

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.200
they have their own special team, there's
an entirely different Spider-Man in the Marvel

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.750
Universe: Miles Morales, who stepped into
the spotlight in 2018’s Into the Spider-Verse,

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.840
a movie based entirely on teaching us that
anyone could be Spider-Man if they never give

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.489
up, including Gwen Stacy, Nic Cage, and…
a talking pig.

00:03:07.489 --> 00:03:10.020
"It can get weirder!"

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:10.640
Organic web-shooters?

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:14.430
One of the most intense arguments you could
overhear in a comic book store back when the

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.849
first Spider-Man movie came out concerned
whether Spider-Man's web-shooters were mechanical

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:20.790
or an organic byproduct of his spider-powers.

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:24.599
Going by the comics, though, it doesn’t
matter what side of the argument you’re

00:03:24.599 --> 00:03:26.390
on, you’re wrong either way.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:30.670
When Sam Raimi's film hit theaters, one of
the few notable changes that it made to Spider-Man's

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:34.170
origin story came with the addition of organic
web-shooters.

00:03:34.170 --> 00:03:38.250
This proved to be so controversial that when
the next movies were announced in 2010, the

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:42.629
decision to use the comics’ mechanical web-shooters
literally made headlines.

00:03:42.629 --> 00:03:45.290
After all, he’d never had organic web-shooters
in the comics.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.099
Or at least, he didn't have them in the comics
before those movies came out.

00:03:49.099 --> 00:03:54.040
After their success, a 2005 storyline called
"The Other" ended with Peter being eaten by

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:56.989
a giant spider and emerging with organic webs.

00:03:56.989 --> 00:04:01.150
Those didn't last either; they inexplicably
disappeared with the back-to-basics approach

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:05.540
of the "Brand New Day" story two years later,
but other Marvel Universe spider-characters

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.540
retained them.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.459
In fact, Cindy Moon, a classmate of Peter's
who was bitten by the same radioactive spider,

00:04:11.459 --> 00:04:15.810
is so capable of spinning her own webs that
her superheroic codename became "Silk."

00:04:15.810 --> 00:04:17.009
Who killed Gwen Stacy?

00:04:17.009 --> 00:04:20.750
One of the defining tragedies of Spider-Man's
life was the death of Gwen Stacy.

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.960
In the '60s, she was the love of Peter Parker's
life, forming a complicated love triangle

00:04:25.960 --> 00:04:31.220
with Mary Jane Watson, and providing the backbone
of the soap opera that underscored his Spidey

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:32.220
superheroics.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.620
In Amazing Spider-Man #121, the Green Goblin
kidnapped her and used her as bait to lure

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.330
Spider-Man into a battle at the top of the
George Washington Bridge, and while the story

00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:45.820
title wasn't revealed until the last page,
“The Night Gwen Stacy Died" lived up to

00:04:45.820 --> 00:04:46.820
its name.

00:04:46.820 --> 00:04:50.650
The Goblin is definitely to blame for Gwen's
murder, he's the one who took her to the top

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:54.729
of the bridge and the one who threw her off,
all in an effort to torture Spider-Man.

00:04:54.729 --> 00:04:59.030
If, however, you really want to get technical
about things, he's not the one responsible

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.030
for what literally killed her.

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:03.960
That sad honor goes to Spider-Man himself.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:07.550
It's easy to miss, but the real tragedy can
be seen in the issue.

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:12.160
When Spider-Man tried to save Gwen by snagging
her leg with a web line as she plunged toward

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:15.990
the Hudson River, the sudden stop proved to
be the fatal moment.

00:05:15.990 --> 00:05:21.570
A small "snap!" sound effect next to Gwen
shows it was a broken neck from Peter's attempt

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:24.699
at saving her that actually ended Gwen's life.

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:25.699
Nobody dies?

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:26.970
Spider-Man doesn't kill.

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:31.729
It's one of the absolute rules of the character,
and unlike other heroes, he's lived up to

00:05:31.729 --> 00:05:37.150
it, too, in over 50 years of comics, Spider-Man
has never actually killed anyone himself.

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:39.040
Except for the one time he did.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:44.259
It happened in the pages of Spider-Man vs.
Wolverine, a 1987 one-shot by Christopher

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:49.470
Priest and Mark Bright that pulled Spidey
into a dark world of international espionage.

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:54.590
The conflict between the two title characters
is largely built around a spy codenamed Charlemagne

00:05:54.590 --> 00:05:58.680
who is described as Wolverine's best friend
from his days as a government operative.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:03.310
In this story, Charlie wants Wolverine to
kill her so she won't be apprehended and spend

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.940
the rest of her life being tortured in a gulag.

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:10.770
When Spider-Man stumbles into this situation,
all he knows is Wolverine is trying to kill

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:11.770
someone.

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:15.531
In the fight that ensues, Spidey unleashes
his full strength on the X-Man, "hitting him

00:06:15.531 --> 00:06:17.670
with blows that would wreck cars."

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:21.840
Sadly, Charlie takes advantage of the brutal
conflict, tricking Spider-Man into hitting

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:25.440
her instead of Wolverine, using him to commit
suicide.

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.660
Peter was understandably traumatized by the
event, and while he and Wolverine have made

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:34.610
peace in the years since, it was a source
of bitterness between them for a long time.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:35.610
The Daily Bugle's freelance photographer

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:39.200
The iconic image of Peter Parker is of a young
man struggling to make ends meet by working

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:44.570
as a freelance photographer, selling snapshots
of his own superheroic exploits to the Daily

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:49.729
Bugle to satisfy J. Jonah Jameson's demand
for pictures of Spider-Man.

00:06:49.729 --> 00:06:53.819
If that's your only concept of what Spidey
does when he's not out fighting crime, however,

00:06:53.819 --> 00:06:55.000
it's time for an update.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.330
The truth is, while Peter worked for the Bugle
for decades in a lot of classic stories, he

00:06:59.330 --> 00:07:02.650
hasn't actually been a freelance photographer
for most of this century.

00:07:02.650 --> 00:07:07.500
Starting in 2001, Pete returned to his old
stomping grounds of Midtown High as a science

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:10.930
teacher, but that proved to be a pretty short-lived
career choice.

00:07:10.930 --> 00:07:14.710
In the years since, he’s had a couple of
science-related jobs, working for a think

00:07:14.710 --> 00:07:19.479
tank called Horizon Labs where he reverse-engineered
his crime-fighting gadgets into consumer goods.

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:24.610
He even founded his own multinational corporation
called Parker Industries, although technically,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:29.039
it was Otto Octavius who founded that company
while he was in Peter's body, and Peter had

00:07:29.039 --> 00:07:32.150
to destroy it to keep it from being taken
over by Nazis.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.150
Comics are complicated.

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.150
Til death (or devil) do they part

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:38.700
One of the more controversial moments in Spider-Man's
comics history involved a deal with the devil.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:40.370
Like, the literal devil.

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:41.560
Not just this guy.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:42.940
"Finish it!"

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:46.160
"From evil!"

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:52.680
After Peter revealed his identity during the
events of Civil War, his family was targeted

00:07:52.690 --> 00:07:57.060
by bad guys who nearly killed Aunt May, which
was a good reminder of why he had the whole

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:58.970
secret identity thing to begin with.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:02.520
Unfortunately for the Parker family, that
sort of reveal isn't the kind of thing you

00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.639
can undo… unless you're willing to make
an infernal bargain with Mephisto.

00:08:06.639 --> 00:08:08.210
Which is exactly what happened.

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:12.139
Rather than selling their souls, however,
Peter and then-wife Mary Jane Watson sold

00:08:12.139 --> 00:08:17.340
their marriage, effectively erasing their
years of wedded bliss from Marvel Comics continuity

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.150
as though it had never happened.

00:08:19.150 --> 00:08:23.759
In the Spider-Man comics, Peter and MJ were
both freed up to pursue new relationships,

00:08:23.759 --> 00:08:26.930
but don't let that fool you into thinking
the marriage is completely gone.

00:08:26.930 --> 00:08:31.530
In order to go along with the comic book storyline,
the Spider-Man newspaper strip did away with

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:32.680
the marriage as well.

00:08:32.680 --> 00:08:38.110
Readers reacted so poorly, however, that in
the strip that ran on May 24, 2009, the elimination

00:08:38.110 --> 00:08:40.950
of the marriage was revealed to have been
a dream.

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:44.850
Ever since, Newspaper Spidey has remained
happily married… or at least as happy as

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:48.480
anyone can be when they spend most of their
time getting knocked unconscious right next

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:49.920
to Dilbert.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.920
The spectacular Spider Parents

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:53.440
Everyone knows that Peter Parker was orphaned
at a young age and raised by Aunt May and

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:54.440
Uncle Ben.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.680
Considering that his surrogate parents were
about as wholesome and salt-of-the-earth as

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.170
people could be, it stands to reason that
Richard and Mary Parker were cut from the

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:02.170
same cloth.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.500
Just average, ordinary folks, right?

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:05.500
Wrong.

00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:10.510
In 1968, it was revealed that when the Parker
Parents died, they were under a cloud of suspicion

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.200
as traitors to their country.

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:15.990
The truth, however, was that they were spies
working for S.H.I.E.L.D., who had infiltrated

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:20.000
the Red Skull's criminal organization before
being killed, without ever getting the chance

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.100
to clear their names.

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:25.740
Yes: In case you missed that, Spider-Man's
parents were spies who were murdered by Captain

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:27.310
America's arch-nemesis.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:31.750
If that's not weird enough, they later turned
up alive, only to be revealed even later to

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:33.300
be robot duplicates.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.130
That's not the end of the Parker family's
secrets, though.

00:09:36.130 --> 00:09:40.280
It turns out that in addition to the parents
he never knew, Spidey also had a long-lost

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.690
older sister, Teresa, who was herself a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:09:43.690 --> 00:09:44.690
agent.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.279
After a team-up in which it was revealed that
her memories of the Parkers were falsely implanted

00:09:48.279 --> 00:09:52.000
by Mephisto, it turned out that was also a
ruse.

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:56.440
She was, in fact, Peter's actual sister after
all, and even pitched in to help him fight

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.020
bad guys using her spy skills.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:59.220
With great power comes great dialogue

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.660
If you've ever read any Spider-Man comic,
or seen him in a movie, or a cartoon, or encountered

00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:07.579
the character pretty much anywhere, then you've
almost certainly seen the motto that guides

00:10:07.579 --> 00:10:09.260
his life: "with great power comes great responsibility."

00:10:09.260 --> 00:10:12.120
As we all know, this was the definitive lesson
that Uncle Ben taught his nephew before he

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:13.120
died …

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:14.120
"And remember…

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:15.120
With great power comes great responsibility."

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.480
...except Ben Parker never actually said those
words.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:24.850
In Amazing Fantasy #15, Spidey's often-referenced
motto is never spoken out loud by any character,

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:26.100
not even Uncle Ben.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:30.550
Instead, it only appears in a caption at the
end of the story as a moral lesson imparted

00:10:30.550 --> 00:10:32.630
by… well, Stan Lee.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:36.959
Spidey himself doesn't even say until 25 years
later in 1987.

00:10:36.959 --> 00:10:41.160
As time went on, though, the phrase became
more and more identified with Ben, largely

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:45.130
because it would be really weird for Spider-Man
to solemnly vow to accept the lesson that

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:49.180
was imparted to him through narration by the
guy who wrote his comics.

